    "narrative": "The offensive outlook for Ghost Ride the WHIP centers on the elite matchup against Feltner, making **Corbin Carroll** (87.14) and **Gabriel Moreno** (71.10) our primary catalysts. **Corbin Carroll** benefits from a massive 19% SP skill boost and a high-leverage #2 slot, while **Mickey Moniak** (75.75) warrants an aggressive start as a high-upside play; he is taking full advantage of a 14% platoon edge against Kelly, Me. We are also leaning on the favorable matchup for **Isaac Paredes** (70.96) and **Brice Turang** (66.40), both of whom are projected to capitalize on high-xERA starters, though **Brice Turang** faces a slight hurdle against the lefty Ray.\n\nOur bench logic was refined by the need to balance daily efficiency against our long-term pursuit of scarcity in the stolen base category. **Max Muncy** (42.29) was the most difficult exclusion, finishing just behind **Travis Bazzana** (42.82) and **Zach McKinstry** (47.30). While **Max Muncy** offers steady production, the optimizer prioritized the speed-on-base potential of **Zach McKinstry**, whose matchup against the slower delivery of Griffin provides a marginal but necessary boost to our SB floor. We remain cautious regarding the \"Lineup Pending\" status for **Cole Young**, **Travis Bazzana**, **Austin Riley**, **Seiya Suzuki**, **Zach McKinstry**, and **Josh Naylor**; if these confirmed batting orders fluctuate significantly, our efficiency scores will require recalibration. \n\nNotably, **Josh Naylor** (62.07) remains a lock in the UTIL spot despite the pending lineup, as his raw power profile overcomes the moderate threat of Warren. We continue to monitor the status of **James Wood**, who remains on the IL, alongside depth pieces like **Vinnie Pasquantino**, **Giancarlo Stanton**, **Trevor Story**, **Dalton Rushing**, and **Edwin Arroyo**, who are all currently positioned outside the daily starting framework.",
